            <ArticleTitle>[The predictive factors of intra-stent restenosis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1035-44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Stenting has contributed to improve the early angiographic result, the restenosis rate and the problem of acute and subacute coronary occlusion. In spite of this, the restenosis phenomenon still remains a problem to be completely solved. The aim of the study was to identify clinical, angiographical and procedural factors that are predictive of in-stent restenosis after successful implantation of coronary stent.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We retrospectively analyzed 202 lesions, in 176 consecutive patients who underwent stent implantation with success in our hospital between January 1995 and August 1998. All patients had a clinical follow-up and an angiography after six months of stent implantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">From 202 lesions evaluated, 47 evolved with restenosis (23%). The only independent predictive variables were: to be receiving hypolipemiant treatment before stenting (OR: 0.3; IC: 0.1-0.8), the use of high pressure for stent implantation (OR: 0.4; IC: 0.2-0.9), to implant stent in &lt; 3.1 mm (OR: 2.2; IC: 1.1-4.5) and to have a residual stenosis &gt; 30% after stenting (OR: 13; IC: 1.5-120).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The only statistical variables associated with in-stent restenosis phenomenon were: be under hypolipemiant treatment before the procedure and the use of high pressures for stent implantation; while risk factors arose: to implant stent in vessels &lt; 3.1 mm and suboptimal angiography result after stenting.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
